{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "ואלה שמות בני ישראל", :translit "v'eleh sh'mot b'nei yisra'el", :gloss "And these are the names of the children of Israel"} {:he "ואדם אשר לומד הסדר", :translit "v'adam asher lomeid ha-seder", :gloss "And a person who studies the order"} {:he "שנים מקרא ואחד תרגום", :translit "shnayim mikra v'echad targum", :gloss "twice Scripture and once translation"} {:he "בקול נעים ישיר יחיה שנים רבות ארוכים לעולם", :translit "b'kol na'im yashir yichyeh shanim rabot aruchot l'olam", :gloss "with a pleasant voice, sings, lives long years, abundant and enduring forever"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :rashei-tevot, :phrase "ואדם אשר לומד הסדר שנים מקרא ואחד תרגום בקול נעים ישיר יחיה שנים רבות ארוכים לעולם", :spells "ואלה שמות בני ישראל"}], :frame {:text "The practice of reviewing each verse of the weekly Torah portion twice in Hebrew and once in Aramaic translation (Targum Onkelos) is prescribed as the minimum regimen for completing one's obligation of Torah study. Berakhot 8a-b records that this was the practice of Rav, who described it as an established obligation for every person. The Shulchan Arukh, Orach Chayim 285:2 codifies it as law, following the principle articulated by the Rosh that one who reads Shnayim Mikra v'Echad Targum each week merits long life.", :sources ["Berakhot 8a-b" "Shulchan Arukh Orach Chayim 285:2"]}} {:quotes [{:he "ואלה שמות בני ישראל הבאים", :translit "v'eleh sh'mot b'nei yisra'el ha-ba'im", :gloss "And these are the names of the children of Israel, the ones coming"} {:he "שביה", :translit "sh'viyah", :gloss "her captivity"} {:he "צפנת פענח", :translit "tzaf'nat pa'ane'ach", :gloss "revealer of hidden things"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :rashei-tevot, :phrase "שביה", :spells "שמות בני ישראל הבאים"} {:kind :other, :note "Vav of ואלה links back to preceding matter, connecting Joseph's death to the preservation of Israelite names"}], :frame {:text "The tradition that Israel was redeemed from Egypt on account of four practices — they did not change their names, their language, did not engage in gossip, and were morally modest — appears in Leviticus Rabbah 32:5, with variant lists in Song of Songs Rabbah 4:12 and Tanchuma, Ki Tisa 6. Genesis 41:45 records Pharaoh renaming Joseph as Tzaf'nat Pa'ane'ach, the basis for the tradition that Joseph instructed his brothers to preserve their Hebrew names despite his own name being changed.", :sources ["Leviticus Rabbah 32:5" "Song of Songs Rabbah 4:12" "Genesis 41:45"]}} {:quotes [{:he "את יעקב איש", :translit "et ya'akov ish", :gloss "[with] Jacob, a man"} {:he "ס\"ת", :translit "sa-mach-tav", :gloss "shabbat (acrostic)"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :rashei-tevot, :phrase "סופי תיבות את יעקב איש", :spells "סת"} {:kind :masorah, :count 2, :occurrences ["ואלה שמות בני ישראל הבאים" "לעולם"]} {:kind :other, :note "Parable of palace-builder: structure that holds only with twelve pillars, corresponding to the twelve tribes"}], :frame {:text "Exodus Rabbah 1:9 and 1:12 records that Israel was redeemed from Egypt because they clung to three practices — they did not change their names, their language, and they observed circumcision. The specific merit of Shabbat observance in Egypt as a cause of redemption appears in the version preserved in Shemot Rabbah 1:10, where Israel is said to have kept the Sabbath to be recognized as a distinct people. The parable of a palace built on one pillar then two then three, falling each time until twelve pillars, appears in Yalkut Shim'oni on Exodus 1:1, interpreting the twelve tribes as the enduring foundation.", :sources ["Exodus Rabbah 1:9" "Exodus Rabbah 1:10" "Exodus Rabbah 1:12" "Yalkut Shim'oni on Exodus 1:1"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :rashei-tevot, :phrase "ואדם אשר לומד הסדר שנים מקרא ואחד תרגום בקול נעים ישיר יחיה שנים רבות ארוכים לעולם", :spells "ואלה שמות בני ישראל", :letters ["ו" "א" "ל" "ה" "ש" "מ" "ו" "ת" "ב" "נ" "י" "י" "ש" "ר" "א" "ל"], :by :selah, :order :in-order} {:kind :rashei-tevot, :phrase "שביה", :spells "שמות בני ישראל הבאים", :letters ["ש"], :by :selah, :order :mismatch} {:kind :other, :note "Vav of ואלה links back to preceding matter, connecting Joseph's death to the preservation of Israelite names", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :rashei-tevot, :phrase "סופי תיבות את יעקב איש", :spells "סת", :letters ["ס" "ת" "א" "י" "א"], :by :selah, :order :mismatch} {:kind :masorah, :count 2, :occurrences ["ואלה שמות בני ישראל הבאים" "לעולם"], :by :selah, :located [{:phrase "ואלה שמות בני ישראל הבאים", :refs ["Genesis 46:8" "Exodus 1:1"], :n 2, :lens :exact} {:phrase "לעולם", :refs ["Exodus 19:9" "Deuteronomy 23:7" "Judges 2:1" "2 Samuel 7:29" "2 Kings 5:27" "2 Kings 21:7" "Isaiah 14:20" "Isaiah 25:2"], :n 155, :lens :exact}]} {:kind :other, :note "Parable of palace-builder: structure that holds only with twelve pillars, corresponding to the twelve tribes", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :exodus, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:13:12.812872709Z", :model "glm-5.2"}